-- title:
--   Integrality over L[j] descends to an intermediate field
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ an $L$-algebra, let $E$ be an intermediate field of $F/L$, i.e. an $L$-subfield of $F$, and let $j, x \in F$ with $hj : j \in E$ and $hx : x \in E$. Assume that $x$, as an element of $F$, is integral over the $L$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ L\ \{j\} = L[j]$ of $F$, that is, $x$ satisfies a monic polynomial with coefficients in $L[j]$. The conclusion is that the element $\langle x, hx\rangle$ of $E$ is integral over the $L$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ L\ \{\langle j, hj\rangle\}$ of $E$ generated by the element $\langle j, hj\rangle$ of $E$, i.e. integrality is transferred from the ambient field $F$ and its subring $L[j] \subseteq F$ to the intermediate field $E$ and the corresponding subring $L[j] \subseteq E$. The two base rings are canonically isomorphic, but they are literally different Lean objects, and the statement records the passage between them.
--
--   A bookkeeping lemma on base-ring transport for integrality: it lets an integral dependence established in a large field $F$ over $L[j]$ be read inside any intermediate field containing $j$ and the element in question. It is used throughout the construction of modular function fields and their places, for instance when elements known to be integral over $\bar{\mathbb Q}[j]$ must be treated as integral elements of a modular function field.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_isIntegral_adjoin_intermediateField_mk.lean

import Mathlib.RingTheory.IntegralClosure.IsIntegral.Basic
import Mathlib.FieldTheory.IntermediateField.Basic

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

theorem AlgebraicCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_intermediateField_mk {L F : Type*} [Field L] [Field F] [Algebra L F] (E : IntermediateField L F) {j x : F} (hj : j ∈ E) (hx : x ∈ E) (h : IsIntegral (Algebra.adjoin L {j}) x) : IsIntegral (Algebra.adjoin L {(⟨j, hj⟩ : E)}) (⟨x, hx⟩ : E) := by sorry
